It has been proved that there is an absolute constant $c$ such that for infinitely many $n$ satisfying $\tau(n+k
)\ll k^c$ for all integers $k\geq1$, by C. F. Lau in [La26] (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.15042).

(The site has been updated to address this comment.)

Currently, Wouter van Doorn points out that for each $a \in A$ there must exist a prime $p$ such that $a^2 + 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p^2}$; and since this is satisfied by exactly two residue classes for each prime $p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$, the density of $A$ is at most
\begin{equation*}
2 \sum_{p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}} \frac{1}{p^2}.
\end{equation*}
Using the inclusion-exclusion principle, van Doorn's above upper bound on the density can be improved to
\begin{equation*}
1 - \prod_{p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}} \left(1 - \frac{2}{p^2}\right).
\end{equation*}

(The site has been updated to address this comment.)

Call such a set Sidon with at most one exception (very original name, I know), and let $F(N)$ be the largest size of such a set within $\{1,...,N\}$. Of course, Erdős and Freud have shown that $(\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}} + o(1))N^{1/2} \leq F(N)$.

I have a nice argument which shows that $F(N) \leq (\sqrt{2} + o(1))N^{1/2}$. If $A$ is Sidon with at most one exception, and $n$ is the unique number that can be represented as a sum in more than one way, then the sets $A \cap [1, n/2]$ and $A \cap (n/2, n]$ are individually Sidon. That is, if $A$ is Sidon with at most one exception, then $A$ can be split into two intervals on which it is Sidon. If we let $h(N)$ be the size of the largest Sidon set in $\{1,...,N\}$, then we therefore have
\begin{equation*}
F(N) \leq \max_k h(k) + h(N - k).
\end{equation*}
It is known (see [30]) that $h(N) = N^{1/2} + o(N^{1/2})$. Using calculus, you can see that for fixed $N$, the value $k^{1/2} + (N - k)^{1/2}$ is never greater than $\sqrt{2}(N^{1/2})$. It follows that $F(N) \leq (\sqrt{2} + o(1))N^{1/2}$.

Nice! I was caught up trying to restrict the semiprimes in ways that satisfy the problem, but you're right - the entire set of semiprimes (excluding primes squared), together with the primes, has Property P.

On sets of squarefree numbers: if we let $f(n)$ be the size of the largest set with Property P in $\{1,...,n\}$, and we let $g(n)$ be the size of the largest such set consisting of squarefree numbers, then we have
\begin{equation*}
(1 + o(1))\frac{n \log \log n}{\log n} \leq g(n) \leq f(n) \leq n.
\end{equation*}
By partitioning $\{1,...,n\}$ according to the largest square factor of a number, we get
\begin{equation*}
f(n) \leq \sum_{k=1}^\infty g\left(\left\lfloor\frac{n}{k^2}\right\rfloor\right).
\end{equation*}
I'm not sure if the current bounds are good enough to do anything with this (for example, I don't currently see a way to prove that $f(n) \ll g(n)$), but I suspect that if you can find a ''nice enough'' asymptotic upper bound for $g(n)$, then it could be combined with the above inequality to give an upper bound for $f(n)$. Erdős does claim that Sárközy proved $f(n) = o(n)$, though I'm not sure if anyone knows of a reference. Perhaps it would be helpful here if one could find it.

Let $A$ be the set of all numbers of the form $pq$, where $p$ and $q$ are primes such that $\pi(p) \not\equiv \pi(q) \pmod{2}$. (That is, one is an odd-indexed prime and the other is an even-indexed prime.) Then $A$ has the desired property. Furthermore, $A$ (asymptotically) contains half the semiprimes, so we have
\begin{equation*}
|A| \geq (\frac{1}{2} + o(1))\frac{n \log \log n}{\log n}.
\end{equation*}

Actually, I realized we can let $A$ be the set of all numbers of the form $pq$ where $p$ and $q$ are primes with $p < \sqrt{n}$ and $q \geq \sqrt{n}$. Then $A$ once again has the desired property and we have
\begin{equation*}
|A| \geq (1 + o(1))\frac{n \log \log n}{\log n}.
\end{equation*}

Some powerful numbers of these forms are:
\begin{equation*}
2^1 - 1 = 1
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
2^3 + 1 = 9 = 3^2
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
2! - 1 = 1
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
4! + 1 = 25 = 5^2
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
5! + 1 = 121 = 11^2
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
7! + 1 = 5041 = 71^2
\end{equation*}
I don't know of any others. Interestingly, half of these are also solutions to [398].

It may also be interesting to see how much the upper bound in [Theorem 2, EPS87] can be improved. Almost trivially, by taking instead
\begin{align*}
l&=\exp\left(c_0((\log x)(\log\log x)(\log\log\log x))^{1/3}\right),\\
L&=\exp\left(c_1((\log x)(\log\log x))^{2/3}(\log\log\log x)^{-1/3}\right),
\end{align*}
with suitable constants $c_0,c_1>0$ in their proof, and using Theorem 1.1 in this paper (https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01214) by Banks et al. (2018) to handle (iv), one can show that the number of $n\le x$ with $\phi(n)=\phi(n+1)$ is at most\[x\exp\left(-c_0((\log x)(\log\log x)(\log\log\log x))^{1/3}\right).\]

